# {{.project_name}}

Skyulf 0.9.1 Bundle: **{{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}}independent target models{{else}}{{.task}}{{end}}** with **{{.engine}}**, using **{{.compute_mode}}**
compute. Spark reads bounded Delta inputs and writes predictions; preprocessing
and model fitting run locally on the selected compute.

## Optional multi-target training

The initializer's `training_layout` defaults to `single_model`. Choose
`multi_target` to use the same `train` job with one `train_models` task; the
project still has exactly two jobs (`train` and `score`). Setup asks shared data,
limits, output selection, compute and training schedule questions. Configure model/target, split,
CV, tuning and quality settings in branches.py; those questions are hidden.
Multi-target keeps `promotion_policy=manual_approval` and `score_handoff=disabled`.
Preflight rejects automatic promotion, scoring handoff, and task repair/retry.

Configure `build_training_branches()` in `src/modeling/branches.py`; its empty
default deliberately fails preflight. The commented example defines two
regressions and one classification with distinct targets, models, feature lists
and comparison metrics. Each branch has a `workflow` overlay on
`config/workflow.json` and optional `features_path` relative to `src/modeling`
(default `../features`). Overlay values replace complete top-level fields:
setting `pipeline` replaces the whole pipeline, with no nested model merging.
Keep its preprocessing list empty; the selected Python recipe fills it.
A branch selects `preprocessing_recipe` and `pre_split_recipe` independently.
Omitting a selector calls that phase's default builder. Unknown names fail early.
Use names such as `{catalog}.{metadata_schema}.revenue{resource_suffix}` to
retain deployment bindings and namespace isolation. Feature packages must stay
inside `src`; keep them under `src/features/`, or add their paths to sync includes.
All modeling Python and feature Python files are explicitly included in sync.

For example, keep all custom implementations in the shared `src/features/custom/`
package and select different ordered step lists:

| Branch | preprocessing_recipe | pre_split_recipe |
| --- | --- | --- |
| revenue | frequency_only | complete_inputs |
| cost | imputer_only | none |
| churn | combined | complete_inputs |

`preprocessing.py` defines `build_preprocessing(recipe="default")` and
`pre_split.py` defines `build_pre_split_steps(recipe="default")`. Add a named
function to either file's mapping to create a recipe. Each function returns a
normal list of Core/custom steps. The starters use `feature_value` and `category`;
adapt those columns to the selected branch's inputs. `frequency_only` performs
no imputation; `combined` runs the imputer then custom frequency encoding.
`complete_inputs` requires at least one of the two inputs to be present.

Shared recipes share code, not fitted values: each branch and CV fold learns its
own statistics from its own training rows. Selected recipe names are bound into
the saved source, so model reload and saved-plan replay never use another branch's
current/default recipe. Pre-split scoring reuse uses the selected pre-split list.
Legacy zero-argument builders remain valid when selectors are omitted.

All branches share one pinned input Delta version and one captured training
clock. Each has independent labels, preprocessing, optional search/CV, registered
candidate, and comparison against its counterpart in the pinned champion set.
Train-only projects use each model's own champion. Keep every known target
column out of all input feature lists. Missing labels are excluded separately
per branch. The report exposes the parent run and every component result.

Multi-target projects enable `src/modeling/model_set.py`. Training packages every
completed component candidate under one model-set name/version/digest. It copies
the frozen component artifacts and shared `src/features/` source into the set.
Component aliases stay unchanged. The set's `promotion_policy` controls its
activation; legacy projects without that factory remain train only.
`src/features/scoring.py` contains both stages: `build_model_rules()` selects each
model's eligibility and output rules; `build_combined_rules()` selects calculations
that use several model results. The latter returns an empty list by default.
To enable one, declare its callback, version, parameters, typed columns and
`required_components` there. The commented profit example depends
only on revenue and cost; an unrelated component's exclusion does not suppress it.
The feature package exports `build_scoring` as the compatible model-rule hook.

### Select published results

The initializer asks `model_set_name`, `model_set_output_mode` and `model_set_table_name` for this
layout. Blank names use project defaults; explicit names use exactly that object
name with the target's catalog/schema and deployment suffix added automatically.
`model_set_name` names the registered model set, not a table (default:
`<project_name>_set`). `model_set_table_name` names its physical result table
(default: `<project_name>_set_scores`). View defaults are
`<project_name>_predictions_<branch>` and `<project_name>_business_results`.
Edit `publication` in `src/modeling/model_set.py` to change the selection:

| Mode | Physical table | Consumer views |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `all` (default) | Model predictions, model outputs and combined business results | None |
| `combined_only` | Combined business results, keys, rule status and set provenance | None |
| `separate_views` | All results saved once | One per selected model, plus combined results when rules exist |

All modes still run each model's saved scoring rules before combined rules.
`combined_only` requires active combined rules in the saved set; configure,
train and approve them first. It omits intermediate predictions from storage.
Switching between this mode and the full schema requires a new table name.

For `separate_views`, setup also asks `model_view_prefix` and
`combined_view_name`. `model_views=None` creates a view for every saved branch;
a mapping selects only its listed branches and gives each a custom name:

```python
"model_views": {
    "revenue": "{catalog}.{output_schema}.revenue_predictions{resource_suffix}",
    "cost": "{catalog}.{output_schema}.cost_predictions{resource_suffix}",
},
"combined_view": "{catalog}.{output_schema}.profit_results{resource_suffix}",
```

`model_views={}` exposes no individual model views. Views contain record keys,
the relevant output/status columns and set provenance; model columns retain their
branch prefix. Views are projections, not additional prediction tables. All read
one committed backing table. A rule error fails the job before any new data is
written; earlier successful results remain. View setup happens before the data
write and can leave empty/old-result views if setup fails. Existing unrelated
objects are never replaced. Changed view sources/projections require new names;
removing a view from configuration does not delete an existing catalog object.

For example, after scoring `id=101`, inserting `id=102` lets the next batch append
its prediction. Updating a feature on `id=101` or deleting that row is different:
the default `source_change_policy="reject"` fails the batch. Choose
`rebuild_on_change` in Bundle setup (or `modeling/model_set.py`) to rebuild the
complete current snapshot with the selected set when UPDATE/DELETE is observed.
Deleted rows disappear, corrected rows and business results are recomputed,
and lag/rolling history starts from the corrected snapshot. Ordinary inserts
still append. The full snapshot must fit `max_rows` and `max_input_mb`; otherwise
the job fails and keeps previous results. CDF/permission failures still fail.

The two questions have independent purposes: `model_change_mode` controls a
new model-set selection; `source_change_policy` controls changed/deleted source
rows. A source-triggered rebuild uses the selected set for ALL rows, even if
earlier rows were scored with older versions in append mode. There is no
retraining. Old projects without the new setting retain `reject`. This option
currently belongs to model-set scoring; the standalone single-model insert
scorer keeps its existing source-change rejection contract.

### Set quality and activation

Bundle setup asks `model_set_promotion_policy` (default `manual_approval`). Edit
`promotion_policy` in `src/modeling/model_set.py` to choose `automatic` later.
The set policy is separate from individual branch policies, which stay manual.
Set each branch's `metric`, `quality_threshold`, optional `quality_gates` and
`min_improvement` in its `workflow` dictionary in `src/modeling/branches.py`.
For example, RMSE with threshold 10 means RMSE <= 10; F1 with threshold 0.8
means F1 >= 0.8. Use limits suitable for your task, including ensemble branches.

The first set needs passing absolute thresholds for every branch. Replacements
also require every model to strictly improve over the corresponding component
in the pinned champion set; ties fail even with min_improvement=0. The quality
policy and baseline are saved with the set and evaluated on saved holdout rows.
Editing branches.py cannot change a previously registered set's policy.
Branch names and registered component names must match the existing set layout.

Automatic mode checks all gates after packaging. A failed gate leaves the current
champion unchanged and reports the failed components. A complete passing set
becomes champion. Missing evidence, changed baselines and execution errors fail
the job. Scoring stays a separate job. Manual approval checks the same gates.

For manual approval, use the `train` job's `lifecycle_action=approve`, the concrete set
`candidate_version`, and `expected_champion_version` (`none` for first activation).
Approval executes the saved complete set on a bounded scoring-source snapshot and
requires each component and rule to produce at least one result, then verifies
every saved component quality policy before changing the set alias.
Old sets without quality pins remain scoreable but need retraining/packaging for
approval through this Bundle flow.
Save the returned promotion receipt. To restore its prior complete set, use
`lifecycle_action=rollback`, `promotion_receipt_json`, and the expected current
champion version.

Every completed training candidate is nominated as the set's `challenger`. A new
candidate moves the displaced contender to `previous_challenger`. Approval removes
the promoted version's challenger alias and preserves the old champion as
`previous_champion`. The individual component aliases remain unchanged.

Use `lifecycle_action=reject` with the current set `candidate_version`,
`expected_champion_version` and `rejection_reason` to record a manual rejection.
The rejected set stays visible as challenger until a new candidate replaces it;
its `approval_status=rejected` prevents later approval. Rejection does not run
training or scoring, or require passing quality gates. An identical repeat reuses
the original receipt. A changed reason, candidate or champion fails explicitly.
Rollback restores the complete prior set and preserves unrelated challenger history.

The same `score` job calls `score_models.py` for this layout. It pins the set
champion once (or uses `score_model_version`) and publishes the full keyed result
with set identity. Scoring loads saved code, so changing editable rules requires
training/packaging and approving a new set. Both jobs remain serialized with
`max_concurrent_runs=1`; they must exclusively own set aliases and output writes.
Append retains earlier rows with their original set version; full rebuild
atomically replaces compatible output on a set change. Temporal carry changes
require full rebuild. Declare rule dependencies as exact pins in
`src/features/requirements.txt`; conflicting component pins are rejected.
The remaining lifecycle instructions below apply to `training_layout=single_model`.

## 1. Configure and preview

| File | Edit here |
| --- | --- |
| `config/workflow.json` | Existing input tables, record keys, features/target, model parameters, validation/search, data selection, limits and promotion/scoring policies |
| `src/features/pre_split.py` | `build_pre_split_steps()` for fixed cleanup and training eligibility |
| `src/features/preprocessing.py` | `build_preprocessing()` for transformations fitted inside each CV fold |
| `src/features/scoring.py` | `build_model_rules()` for each model; `build_combined_rules()` for calculations across models |
| `src/features/assets.json` | Relative paths of bounded data files to save with the model |
| `src/features/requirements.txt` | Exact project dependency pins verified at fit/load and recorded by MLflow |
| `src/features/custom/` | Optional Calculator/Applier pairs and helper modules; use relative imports |
| `src/modeling/tuning.py` | Optional `build_search_space()` override; returning `None` uses Core defaults |
| `src/modeling/ensemble.py` | Optional voting/stacking composition; returning `None` keeps JSON parameters and Core defaults |
| `src/jobs/` | Notebook entrypoints referenced by the two jobs |
| `src/tools/preview.py` | Offline configuration preview and installed-node discovery |
| `databricks.yml` | Workspace/UC bindings for `dev`, `test`, `syst`, `prod`; schedule and compute variables |
| `resources/train.job.yml` | Training, comparison, approval/rollback and optional scoring handoff |
| `resources/score.job.yml` | Batch scoring job and its independent schedule/compute |

`databricks.yml` includes both job files through `resources/*.yml`. Keep the
resource keys `train` and `score` stable; the training handoff references
`${resources.jobs.score.id}` across files. When updating an older generated
project, replace `resources/workflow.jobs.yml` with these two files; do not
leave all three present. The job definitions and resource keys stay the same.

Training captures every Python file under `src/features/` in one versioned
64 KiB snapshot. Relative imports keep custom modules available after MLflow
reload, even if editable project files change. Add `__init__.py` to subpackages.
The `src/features/assets.json` file contains detailed `_help` instructions and an
initially empty `files` list. Create the data file yourself, then declare it as
`"files": ["assets/bands.json"]`. The legacy `["assets/bands.json"]` format also
works. Assets can be used by pre-split, preprocessing and scoring; listing one
does not enable a step. Read it from saved custom code with
`read_project_asset(__package__, "assets/bands.json")` from
`skyulf.inference.project_package`. Code, encoded assets and dependency pins
share the 64 KiB snapshot budget. Paths must remain inside the feature package;
asset symlinks are rejected. Files outside the manifest are not embedded.

Use exact `distribution==version` pins in `src/features/requirements.txt`.
Install these in the training/scoring job environments as well: the loader
verifies versions and never installs packages during prediction. MLflow exports
the pins in its environment requirements. Changing code/assets/pins requires
a new model version. Large external model downloads are outside this contract.

Custom implementations live in two modules. Their step calls appear directly
inside the parent builders' returned lists, alongside the Core steps:

| Parent recipe | Inline custom step |
| --- | --- |
| `src/features/pre_split.py` | `minimum_completeness(columns=["a", "b", "c"], min_present=2)` |
| `src/features/preprocessing.py` | `frequency_encoding(columns=["category"])` |

Uncomment the corresponding import and step, then adapt its column names.
The list defines execution order; custom factories return normal Core step
dictionaries. There are no separate column-selection variables or enable
switches. Keep the JSON preprocessing and pre_split_steps lists empty.

For `minimum_completeness(columns=["a", "b", "c"], min_present=2)`, rows with at least
two observed fields survive unchanged. Null/NaN are missing; blanks/infinity are
values. Filter fields need not become model inputs. Training filters do not run
on scoring rows; use only fields known at the observation cutoff.

For `frequency_encoding(columns=["category"])`, training `["A", "A", "B", null]` learns
A=0.5 and B=0.25. Missing/new categories map to zero during inference. Each CV
fold fits its own mapping; the saved model reuses its trained frequencies.
Other columns and row order stay unchanged. Select string/null categorical
features in input_columns; do not select target or record-key columns.

Preview the configured project:

```bash
python src/tools/preview.py --action train
```

Review source column names and positive `max_rows`/`max_input_mb` budgets.
Overflow fails. `training_version: null` pins the latest snapshot at run start;
an explicit integer pins that Delta version. Random full-snapshot training needs
no dates; temporal training needs an event column and a valid window.
Snapshot/split, sampling and CV details use defaults without setup
questions. Override them in init JSON or the generated config; preview after edits.

The generated project searches parameters for the selected model, then refits
the winner. To set fixed base parameters, edit
`pipeline.modeling.base_model.params` in `config/workflow.json`, or provide
`model_params` as a JSON object in an init file. Voting and stacking
models require composition settings such as `base_estimators`; setup asks for
`model_params` when one is selected. Use `--list-models` to inspect available
models and their parameter metadata.

For voting or stacking, `src/modeling/ensemble.py:build_ensemble_params(model_type)`
receives the selected registered model type and can return a JSON object of
ensemble parameters. Its keys override matching keys in
`pipeline.modeling.base_model.params`; other JSON parameters remain. The default
returns `None`. Set `USE_EXAMPLES = True` in that file to try its four editable
classification/regression voting/stacking recipes. Training and preview capture
the exact recipe source and digest before the tuning hook runs; scoring loads the
saved fitted artifact. Existing projects without `ensemble.py` still work.

For example, a voting regression model can use
`{"base_estimators":["linear_regression","ridge"],"base_estimator_params":{"ridge":{"alpha":2.0}}}`
as `model_params`. A stacking model can additionally set `final_estimator`,
`final_estimator_params`, `cv` and `passthrough` in the same object. Choose
component keys valid for the selected classification or regression model.
Optional classifier calibration uses `calibrate_base_models`,
`calibration_method` (`sigmoid` or `isotonic`) and `calibration_cv`. Stacking also
has its own internal `cv` for the final learner; this is separate from the
workflow's candidate-scoring `cv_*` settings. Keep `n_jobs: 1` to avoid nested
parallel fits. `tune_base_models` opts into the selected base learners' search
axes; leave it off to search only the ensemble's own parameters.

The generated config uses Core's `hyperparameter_tuner` wrapper. The Core model
registry supplies a search space for the selected model and strategy. For a project
override, edit `src/modeling/tuning.py:build_search_space()` and return finite candidate
lists, or provide `search_space` as a JSON object in an init file. The generated
`pipeline.modeling.search_space: {}` requests Core defaults. The checked-in
`skyulf-core/templates/databricks/examples/tuning-init.example.json` shows a
bounded random search using the model's default axes. After selecting a strategy,
choose **Default** for Core controls or **Custom** for its relevant controls.
Grid and halving-grid evaluate the full Cartesian space and fail preflight when
it exceeds `max_candidates` (default 1000, maximum 10000); raise that limit or
narrow the space. Random, halving-random and Optuna use `n_trials` (maximum 1000)
as a candidate budget. Halving supports `factor`, `min_resources`,
and `resource`; `n_estimators` resource also needs numeric starting and maximum
budgets and a compatible estimator. The resource cannot also be a search axis;
remove `n_estimators` from the search space with `src/modeling/tuning.py` when using that
resource. Optuna needs `optuna` and `optuna-integration` in the
training environment; its timeout is a soft stop between fits, not a hard
interruption of a running estimator.

The top-level `cv_*` fields are the only fold settings. They score search
candidates; `cv_enabled: false` uses an internal training-only validation split. There is
no second post-search CV report. The search objective (`rmse`, `accuracy`, etc.)
is separate from the final holdout comparison metric (`heldout_rmse`,
`heldout_accuracy`, etc.). `search_random_state`, `cv_random_state`, and the
top-level split `random_state` control different randomness. Core's `nested_cv`
option repeats tuning independently inside each outer training fold. `cv_folds`
sets outer folds; optional `cv_inner_folds` sets inner folds. A separate final
search selects the saved model. Each search gets the configured trial/time
budget. Outer evaluation and final-search scores are reported separately.
The final holdout is reserved for model comparison.

`cv_nested_type` chooses `auto`, `k_fold`, `stratified_k_fold`,
`time_series_split`, `group_k_fold` or `stratified_group_k_fold` at both nested
levels. Temporal CV uses `event_column` and `cv_shuffle: false`; `cv_gap`,
`cv_test_size` and `cv_max_train_size` are row counts (null maximum expands the
training window). Nested temporal CV requires a temporal final holdout. Missing
times and timestamps tied across fold boundaries fail explicitly.

Group CV uses `cv_group_column` only as split metadata. Keep it out of
`input_columns`. Random final holdout splits whole groups; set `stratify: false`.
A temporal final holdout must also have disjoint groups. Missing groups fail.

For binary nested search, set `pipeline.modeling.tune_threshold: true` to select
thresholds from inner out-of-fold probabilities. Outer and final holdout labels
remain untouched. The saved artifact applies the independent final threshold;
`tuning.json` and the readable results retain threshold evidence. Models without
probabilities and multiclass threshold tuning fail explicitly.

The preprocessing and tuning recipes start empty; `ensemble.py` has disabled
examples to adapt. Keep `pipeline.preprocessing` and `pre_split_steps` empty in
JSON. Put custom pairs in the features package and enable them in the appropriate
builder. Approval and scoring use saved code, even after project source changes.
See [setup and advanced inputs][setup] and [custom recipes][recipes].

Install the matching Skyulf wheel in your local environment, then from this project:

```powershell
python src/tools/preview.py --action train
python src/tools/preview.py --list-models
python src/tools/preview.py --list-preprocessors
```

Preview executes your trusted recipe builders and checks configuration; it does
not read source data or train. Keep data access/training out of imports and
builders. For another target, pass `--catalog`, `--input-schema`, `--output-schema`,
`--metadata-schema` and `--resource-suffix` using that target's bindings.

## 2. Build and deploy

From the Skyulf checkout, build into this project's `dist` directory:

```powershell
uv build --wheel --no-build-isolation --out-dir <this-project>/dist skyulf-core
```

From this generated project, with your authenticated CLI profile:

```powershell
databricks bundle validate --strict -t dev --profile <profile>
databricks bundle deploy -t dev --profile <profile>
```

`dev` uses your profile's workspace and initialized UC bindings. Replace the
placeholder hosts/catalogs for other targets before using them. The source tables
must already exist; enable Delta Change Data Feed on the scoring source before
new rows arrive. The first score processes all existing rows in that source.
Record keys must be unique, nonnull STRING/BIGINT columns.

{{if eq .compute_mode "policy_cluster"}}
Review the approved cluster policy, runtime, node type and cost tag for each
workspace. See the checkout's `skyulf-core/templates/databricks/examples/policy-init.example.json`.
{{else}}
The jobs use serverless Python environment 4. Policy clusters are a separate
initialization choice.
{{end}}

Deployment creates two jobs and uploads code/configuration; input tables are
not created. Selected schedules start `UNPAUSED` unless you set their Bundle
pause variables to `PAUSED`. Training and scoring clocks are independent.
See [deployment and schedules][schedules] and [existing-project migration][migration].

## 3. Train, inspect and approve

```powershell
databricks bundle run -t dev --profile <profile> train
databricks bundle run -t dev --profile <profile> score
```

Run `score` after a model is selected. When handoff is enabled, first inspect its
child score run; a separate score invocation is only needed for new rows or recovery.

The `train` job has one active run, with queued training and operator actions:

| Task | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `initialize_run` | Validate settings and pin source version, recipe and expected champion |
| `choose_action` | Select training or an explicit approve/reject/rollback action |
| `load_data` | Read the bounded pinned source and save its verified dataset |
| `prepare_dataset` | Apply fixed cleanup and eligibility rules, then save training/holdout partitions |
| `train_and_tune` | Fit preprocessing inside training folds, run configured CV/tuning, and save the fitted pipeline |
| `select_best_model` | Verify the candidate; currently reports one candidate, without multi-model competition |
| `register_model` | Check heldout evaluation before registering and nominating the candidate |
| `evaluate_model` | Compare the registered candidate with the pinned champion on the same holdout |
| `model_decision` | Apply promotion policy or execute an explicit approve/reject/rollback action |
| `training_report` | Finalize the run and publish verified results and next actions |
| `scoring_requested` | Check whether scoring was requested |
| `run_batch_scoring` | Invoke the existing score job |

The separate score job has the `score` task. Open **`training_report`** for
metrics, the decision and ready-to-copy `next_actions`. Search details are in
`train_and_tune`; champion comparison is in `evaluate_model`. Score results are
in the child or independent score run. Manual actions skip data and training
and use `model_decision` directly. Finalization can run after failure; that
does not imply a successful result.

`prepare_dataset` applies only fixed transformations and filters. Learned
preprocessing and feature engineering stay inside `train_and_tune` so each CV
fold learns from its own training rows. With tuning disabled, that task fits
the configured model directly. `select_best_model` currently receives a single
candidate; multi-model competition is a separate planned feature.

Bounded source and split datasets are saved as Parquet artifacts under the
MLflow run's `lifecycle/data/`. Downstream training verifies their saved digests
and preserves split membership without rereading the source. These artifacts
contain training data: apply the experiment's access and retention policy.
Registration and approval retain their source/evidence replay checks.

This project's policies are:

- `score_model_selection={{.score_model_selection}}`: pinned version or champion.
- `promotion_policy={{.promotion_policy}}`: manual approval or automatic gates.
- `score_handoff={{.score_handoff}}`: disabled or score after an alias change.

Set a numeric `quality_threshold` before the first champion approval/promotion.
Later promotions also need positive improvement meeting `min_improvement`.
Training a candidate never changes a saved scoring pin. Only the serialized train
job identity should write lifecycle aliases; enforce exclusive ownership externally.

Use the train job's **Run now parameters** for operator actions:

| `lifecycle_action` | Required inputs |
| --- | --- |
| `train` | Clear operator evidence fields |
| `approve` | Copy `next_actions.approve`: candidate and expected champion |
| `reject` | Copy `next_actions.reject` and add `rejection_reason` |
| `rollback` | Copy `next_actions.rollback`, including `promotion_receipt_json` |

Use explicit `none` for a first champion. Its initialization has no prior champion
to roll back to. A later completed promotion returns rollback inputs. Evidence
is revalidated; editing a registry alias directly is not approval.
See [results and next actions][results] and [operator walkthrough][operators].

## 4. Score new rows and recover

The independent score job accepts `score_model_version` for a one-run concrete
version override; blank uses the configured selector. Handoff uses the configured
selector. `incremental_append` keeps old predictions and scores new inserts;
`full_rebuild` builds a versioned generation and switches the view after success.
An unchanged replay is a no-op. [Prediction behavior and model changes][predictions].

If scoring fails after promotion, retry score independently. Inspect saved
MLflow evidence and registry state before a fresh lifecycle run; lifecycle repair
and retry are rejected because effects may already have committed.
See [failure evidence and pinned replay][replay] and [recovery][schedules].

Keep `workflow_contract: "3"` fixed in the generated notebook parameters.
`config_version: 1` is the separate workflow JSON schema version. Changing
`score_handoff` requires updating `deployed_score_handoff` wherever present in
`resources/train.job.yml` and `resources/score.job.yml`, then redeploying the
matching graph/notebooks/wheel.

